    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>The best side hustles in Minneapolis follow the seasons. Winter dumps snow on thousands of driveways and sidewalks that someone has to clear; summer turns the Chain of Lakes, the riverfront and the stadium district into a nonstop event calendar. In between, one of the densest clusters of Fortune 500 headquarters in the country keeps well-paid professionals busy enough to outsource chores, tutoring and freelance projects.</p>
<p>This guide walks through 18 side gigs that work in Minneapolis right now, with typical pay, startup costs and the local detail (neighborhoods, employers, events and city rules) that makes each one pay. If you want a dependable second income, the trick is simple: pair one cold-weather hustle with one warm-weather hustle so you never have a dead season.</p>

<div class="stat-row">
  <div class="stat"><b>5.35%–9.85%</b><span>Minnesota income tax brackets (2026)</span></div>
  <div class="stat"><b>$15–$75</b><span>typical hourly range across these gigs</span></div>
  <div class="stat"><b>$0–$400</b><span>startup cost for most options</span></div>
</div>

<h2 id="why-minneapolis">Why side hustles in Minneapolis pay off</h2>
<p>A few things set Minneapolis apart from other Midwest cities:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Corporate money.</strong> Target and U.S. Bancorp are headquartered downtown, Ameriprise and Xcel Energy are nearby, and UnitedHealth Group, Best Buy and General Mills sit in the suburbs. Those employers pay well, and their employees buy convenience.</li>
  <li><strong>Winter is a business.</strong> Minneapolis requires homeowners to clear public sidewalks after a snowfall, and the city's snow emergency rules mean cars get towed if they're on the wrong side of the street. That creates real, recurring demand for anyone with a shovel, a blower or a truck.</li>
  <li><strong>A stacked summer.</strong> Art-A-Whirl in Northeast, Twin Cities Pride, the Aquatennial and the Minnesota State Fair (late August through Labor Day, just over the line in Falcon Heights) bring crowds that need rides, staff, parking and food.</li>
  <li><strong>Big student population.</strong> The University of Minnesota's Twin Cities campus is one of the largest in the country, which means constant demand for tutoring, moving help and cheap delivery labor, and a deep pool of student hustlers.</li>
</ul>

