  </tbody>
</table>

<h2 id="top-picks">The best side hustles in Minneapolis: top picks</h2>
<p>These eight combine strong local demand with low startup costs. Start here.</p>
<div class="hustle-grid">
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="snow-removal">1. Snow removal and sidewalk shoveling</h3>
    <p>This is the signature Minneapolis side hustle. Sell season contracts in October to neighbors in Linden Hills, Kenny, Tangletown or Longfellow, and to elderly homeowners who can't shovel. Corner lots and long sidewalks pay more. A good [[amazon:snow-shovel|ergonomic snow shovel]] and ice melt get you started; a two-stage blower lets you take a full route.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $30–$80 per driveway, season contracts $300–$700</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $40–$600</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Early risers who don't mind cold</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="rideshare">2. Rideshare driving</h3>
    <p>MSP Airport runs before dawn, Vikings games at U.S. Bank Stadium, Twins games at Target Field, Timberwolves and Lynx at Target Center, and bar close in the North Loop and Uptown are the money windows. Snowstorms also spike demand from people who won't drive themselves. Our <a href="/lyft-uber/">Lyft and Uber guide</a> covers sign-up.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $16–$28/hr before gas and wear</li><li><b>Startup:</b> Eligible car, winter tires recommended</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Night owls and event weekends</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="delivery">3. Food and grocery delivery</h3>
    <p>DoorDash, Uber Eats and Instacart get busiest on frigid nights when nobody wants to go out. Downtown high-rises and the North Loop stack orders tightly; Edina, St. Louis Park and Minnetonka tend to tip better on grocery runs. Summer bike delivery along the Midtown Greenway works well too.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $15–$24/hr before gas</li><li><b>Startup:</b> Car, bike or e-bike</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Flexible evenings</li></ul>
  </div>
</div>
<p>Before you commit to driving through a Minnesota winter, run the real numbers on gas, tires and the mileage deduction:</p>
<p>[[tool:gig-calculator]]</p>
<div class="hustle-grid">
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="lawn-care">4. Lawn care and spring and fall cleanup</h3>
    <p>The growing season is short, so homeowners want it done right. Weekly mowing runs from roughly May to October, with big paydays for spring dethatching and fall leaf cleanup before the first snow. Pair a lawn route with your snow route and you keep the same clients all year.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $35–$65 per lawn; cleanups $150–$400</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $0–$400</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Snow-route operators</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="pet-care">5. Pet sitting and dog walking</h3>
    <p>Apartment dogs in the North Loop, Loring Park and Northeast need midday walks, and suburban families traveling for hockey tournaments need overnight sitters. Winter walkers who show up in a polar vortex earn loyal clients fast. Rover and Wag are the easy start; repeat clients often go direct.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $20–$30/walk, $45–$80/night</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $0</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Animal lovers with daytime gaps</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="freelancing">6. Freelancing for corporate and agency teams</h3>
    <p>Minneapolis has a deep bench of retail, healthcare, finance and medical-device companies plus a strong ad agency scene. That means steady overflow work in copywriting, design, data analysis and marketing ops. Build a portfolio on <a href="/fiverr/">Fiverr</a> or Upwork, then pitch local agencies directly.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $25–$80/hr</li><li><b>Startup:</b> Laptop</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Writers, designers, analysts</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="tutoring">7. Tutoring and test prep</h3>
    <p>Families in Edina, Wayzata and Minnetonka invest heavily in math, science and ACT prep. U of M grad students can tutor undergrads in-person near Dinkytown and Stadium Village, and anyone with a teaching background can earn online.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $25–$65/hr</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $0–$50</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Teachers and grad students</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="handyman">8. Handyman work for older homes</h3>
    <p>Minneapolis is full of century-old houses in Powderhorn, Kingfield and Northeast with drafty windows, sticky doors and storm windows that need swapping each season. Weatherstripping, furniture assembly and TV mounting are steady small jobs. Leave electrical and plumbing to licensed pros.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $40–$75/hr</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $100–$400 in tools</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Handy people with a car</li></ul>
  </div>
</div>
<div class="callout"><strong>Quick tip:</strong> Snow emergency nights are some of the best-paying hours for delivery and rideshare in the city. Sign up for the City of Minneapolis snow emergency alerts so you know when parking chaos is coming and where demand will spike.</div>

<h2 id="seasonal">Seasonal side gigs in Minneapolis</h2>
<p>The calendar here is predictable, so plan around it.</p>
<div class="hustle-grid">
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="holiday-lights">9. Holiday light installation</h3>
    <p>Nobody wants to be on a ladder in December in Minnesota. Install lights in early November before the ground freezes, then come back in January to take them down. Two visits per client, often booked year after year.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $200–$600 per home</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $150–$300 (ladder, clips)</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Fall income between lawn and snow</li></ul>
  </div>
  <div class="hustle-card">
    <h3 id="event-staffing">10. Event and stadium staffing</h3>
    <p>Concession operators and staffing agencies hire for U.S. Bank Stadium, Target Field, Huntington Bank Stadium, the Minneapolis Convention Center and the State Fair. Many nonprofits also run fair food stands and split proceeds with volunteers.</p>
    <ul class="meta"><li><b>Pay:</b> $16–$25/hr, plus tips at some stands</li><li><b>Startup:</b> $0</li><li><b>Best for:</b> Students and weekend earners</li></ul>
  </div>
