<h2 id="year-plan">A year-round Minneapolis side hustle plan</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>December to March:</strong> snow removal, storm-night delivery, bookkeeping and tutoring.</li>
  <li><strong>April to May:</strong> spring cleanups, bike tune-ups, car detailing to wash off the salt.</li>
  <li><strong>June to August:</strong> lawn routes, event staffing, moving help, wedding photography.</li>
  <li><strong>September to November:</strong> leaf cleanup, State Fair and football shifts, holiday light installs, then sell snow contracts.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="get-started">How to make money in Minneapolis starting this month</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Pick one seasonal and one year-round hustle.</strong> For example, snow removal plus pet sitting, or lawn care plus freelancing.</li>
  <li><strong>Set a monthly target.</strong> Work backward to hours per week with our <a href="/tools/income-calculator/">income calculator</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Get listed where locals look.</strong> Nextdoor, neighborhood Facebook groups and a Google Business Profile for services; the apps for gig work.</li>
  <li><strong>Pre-sell the season.</strong> Sell snow contracts in October and lawn contracts in March, so you know your income before the weather turns.</li>
  <li><strong>Gear up for winter driving.</strong> Winter tires, a scraper and an emergency kit protect your car and your earnings.</li>
  <li><strong>Separate your money.</strong> Open a dedicated account and save receipts from day one.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="minnesota-taxes">Minnesota taxes and Minneapolis rules for side income</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>State income tax:</strong> Minnesota uses four brackets for 2026: 5.35%, 6.80%, 7.85% and 9.85%. For single filers, the 5.35% rate covers taxable income up to $33,310, and most side income will land in the 6.80% bracket. Minneapolis has no separate city income tax.</li>
  <li><strong>Self-employment tax:</strong> 15.3% federal on net profit, on top of federal income tax. Estimate your set-aside with the <a href="/tools/self-employment-tax-calculator/">self-employment tax calculator</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Estimated payments:</strong> if you expect to owe a meaningful amount at tax time, make quarterly federal and Minnesota estimated payments to avoid penalties.</li>
  <li><strong>Sales tax:</strong> the Minnesota state rate is 6.875%, and Minneapolis and Hennepin County add local taxes on top. If you sell taxable goods, register with the Minnesota Department of Revenue. Many personal services are not taxed, but some are, so check before you start.</li>
  <li><strong>Licenses:</strong> most freelance and personal-service work doesn't need a general city license, but short-term rentals must be licensed with the city, and food sales and regulated trades need permits.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout-warn"><strong>Watch out:</strong> Lodging is taxed heavily in Minneapolis. Short-term rental hosts may owe state sales tax plus county and city lodging taxes, so confirm the platform is collecting them before you co-host or list a place.</div>

<h2 id="nearby">Compare with nearby cities</h2>
<p>Curious how the Twin Cities stack up? See our guides to the <a href="/best-side-hustles-in-milwaukee/">best side hustles in Milwaukee</a>, <a href="/best-side-hustles-in-chicago/">Chicago</a> and <a href="/best-side-hustles-in-omaha/">Omaha</a>. Minneapolis stands out for how cleanly its seasons stack, and Better Side Hustles readers here who plan around winter tend to out-earn the ones who just wait for it to end.</p>
<p>Pick one idea, give it a full season of consistent effort and track every hour and dollar. That's how side gigs in Minneapolis turn into reliable income.</p>
